{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 240,
    "dDurationMs": 2640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to AnyDesk for supporting PBS"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2880,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To observe the quantum nature of gravity and of \nspaceime itself, we need a particle collider the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 8880,
    "dDurationMs": 13280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "size of the solar system. Or we could just physics \nsmarter and build one on a lab bench. Here's how."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 22160,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hey everyone—before we start, we need your help. \nThe algorithm is hiding episodes from subscribers.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 30080,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "From past experiments, we know the best way \nto help is to like the episode AND leave a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 37440,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "comment. That tells the algorithm to share it with \nmore of our community. And if you haven’t yet,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 43200,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "subscribe and hit the bell so you don’t miss new \nepisodes. You’re an amazing community, and we want  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 48800,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to keep delivering great content to everyone. \nThanks for your help. Now, on to the episode."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 54800,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The quantum world is certainly strange. Objects \nin multiple places and states at once. Random  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 59920,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "transitions between states and places. weird \ninstantaneous communication over large distances.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 65760,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "All that good quantum weirdness. But perhaps the \nstrangest thing about quantum mechanics is that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 71920,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its rules seem so different from the classical \nlarge scale world. And yet the latter comes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 79200,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from the former. But how? And at what size does \nthat happen? A related mystery is the connection  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 86640,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "between gravity and the quantum. Gravity seems \nmore in line with the classical world. It is  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 92880,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "crisply defined and very non-random. But gravity \nis the fabric of spacetime which is knit together  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 100640,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on scales far smaller than the quantum. So at \nwhat point does spaceime itself become quantum?  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107520,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The question of the quantum classical transition \nand the related quantum gravity connection are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 112880,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "notoriously difficult to probe directly due to the \nchallenge of accessing the minuscule quantum world  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 119520,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the minuscular quantum gravity world. But \nmaybe the answer is not to build tiny experiments  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 127120,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or giant colliders, but to make the quantum world \nbig enough to meet gravity and the classical world  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 135040,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "halfway, perhaps inside a regular laboratory. The \nknown laws of physics can be pretty clearly broken  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 141200,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "up into these two realms, those describing the \nlarge scale classical world and the tiny quantum  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 146960,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "world. Classical physics works great above about \na micrometer where objects are made of many many  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 152960,
    "dDurationMs": 9760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "atoms. Everything larger is as we say macroscopic. \nQuantum mechanics rules below the nanometer scale.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 162720,
    "dDurationMs": 8880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At single molecules and atoms and smaller but the \nin between scale, the meos scale feels both of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 171600,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "these worlds. It is, as we say, semiclassical, \nbehaving mostly classically in some ways,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 177200,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but exhibiting quantum effects in certain \nconditions and when carefully coaxed. Today,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 182400,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we're going to look at approaches and some example \nexperiments for probing fundamental questions  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 189360,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by looking at this intermediate space where both \nclassical and quantum mechanics are at play. Maybe  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 195680,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we don't have to wait for that solar system size \nparticle accelerator to do this cool stuff. We'll  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 201280,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "look at two paths. One is to look for a breakdown \nin our classical understanding of gravity at the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 207200,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mesos scale. The second will be to look for true \nquantum effects in as large a system as possible,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 214720,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but also in the mesos scale. So, we're going to \ntry to make gravity small and then make quantum  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 220320,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "big. If we can get them to meet somewhere in the \nmiddle, maybe we can even make gravity quantum.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 227040,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let's start with an experiment so elegant and \nsimple that it's been our go-to for measuring  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 232720,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the strength of gravity since we well first tried \nto measure the strength of gravity. When Newton  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 238240,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "first wrote down his law of universal gravitation, \nhe reasoned his way to the proportionalities based  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 243920,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on a falling apple and the orbit of the moon. \nThe force of gravity had to be proportional to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 250720,
    "dDurationMs": 8000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the product of those masses divided by the square \nof their separation. But he had no idea of the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 258720,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "value of the constant of proportionality, \nthe gravitational constant. For that,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 263040,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "he would have needed to know the Earth's mass. But \nhow do you weigh the Earth? The answer is that you  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 269360,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "first measure G with known masses and then work \nyour way back to the planet. But that requires us  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 274960,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to measure the minuscule gravitational attraction \nbetween masses that we can make and then weigh.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 282080,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And it wasn't for another century after Newton's \nlaw that this became remotely possible. The guy  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 287920,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "who did it should have been John Mitchell, the \nmost underrated of the geniuses of physics.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 294560,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is the same guy who first conceived of the \nNewtonian version of the black hole, the dark  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 299040,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "star. Mitchell's idea was to measure the minuscule \ngravitational pull between a pair of metal spheres  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 306080,
    "dDurationMs": 7840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "using a remarkable device, the torsion pendulum. \nThis cutting device suspends a rod on a thin wire.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 313920,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Rotating the rod twists the wire leading to a \nrestoring force that pulls it back. This can  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 318880,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "produce pendulum-like oscillations. But it can \nalso be used to measure the strength of the force  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 324000,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "doing the initial displacement. The restoring \nforce increases with twist. So more twist means  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 330560,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more force. And the torsion pendulum is almost \ncompletely free from friction. With a thin wire,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 338080,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it can be sensitive to extremely tiny forces. \nPerfect for the minuscule gravitational force  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 343760,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exerted between non-planet-sized objects. Mitchell \nactually built his torched pendulum, but he died  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 351200,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "before he could complete the experiment. His \nclose friend Henry Cavendish inherited the device,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 357280,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "refined the design, and saw the experiment \nthrough. It looked something like this. Two lead  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 362400,
    "dDurationMs": 8640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "balls of around a kilo uh were fixed to the arms \nof the pendulum while a pair of 160 kg balls were  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 371040,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fixed near these two with the intention of them \ngravitationally attracting the smaller balls. The  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 378000,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "amount of twist in the pendulum would measure that \nforce and because all the masses and distances  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 383040,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "were known, the gravitational constant should \nfall out as the last unknown in the equation.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 388560,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The experimental design and the care taken \nwere extraordinary. So much so that in 1798,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 395680,
    "dDurationMs": 7920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Cavendish measured the gravitational constant to \nwithin 1% of the best modern value. Fun side fact,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 403600,
    "dDurationMs": 7200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Caendish described his experiment as weighing \nthe Earth because with G in hand, we could now  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 410800,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "calculate the Earth's actual mass just from the \ngravitational acceleration at the surface. In the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 416160,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "225 plus years since Cavendish's measurement, \nthis experiment has been greatly refined,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 423280,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but we've improved on that first measurement only \nby about one more decimal place because Cavendish  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 429920,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and Mitchell did such a good job. However, we're \nalso now able to do this experiment with much  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 436320,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "smaller masses, perhaps even small enough \nto see if the rules change as we approach  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 443200,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the quantum realm. And there are some reasons \nto think they might. In a previous episode,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 448320,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we talked about how gravity might deviate from \nthe Newtonian inverse square law at very short  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 455120,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "distances if there are extra coiled up dimensions \non those scales. Essentially, gravity leaks  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 462800,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into those dimensions very close to the source, \ncausing it to weaken faster. But at longer ranges,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 468880,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it settles into the regular inverse square fading. \nBut there are other reasons to think that gravity  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 474480,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "might behave this way. In string theory, there are \nways for gravity to behave like the quantum forces  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 480400,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "whose coupling strength changes with distance due \nto self-shielding. There's also the hypothetical  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 487840,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "chameleon field, which is a dark energy candidate \nthat has a distance dependent variable mass that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 494240,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "could do something similar. And give a theorist \nenough chalk and a sbatical, I'm sure that they'll  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 500080,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "come up with several more options. But how to \ntest? The problem is performing a Cavendish  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 506240,
    "dDurationMs": 8080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "type experiment with quantum or even meoscopic \nmasses brings with it a new slew of noise and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 514320,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "possible interfering effects that not even \nCavendish's dedication to precision could  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 519760,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "overcome. For starters, we know the gravitational \nforce is absurdly weak between two small masses  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 524720,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "when compared to the other forces of nature. For \nexample, the gravitational attraction between two  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 529040,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "electrons is about 42 orders of magnitude weaker \nthan the repulsive electromagnetic force pushing  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 534960,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "them apart, making it essentially impossible to \nmeasure gravity. In that case, we can use neutral  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 541440,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "masses, but even those tend to have internal \nelectric charge distributions that can lead to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 546560,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "dipole interactions when objects are close by. And \nwhen surfaces get very close, the Casmir force and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 553200,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Vanderwal's force come into play, typically \nwith much greater strength than gravity. Even  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 558560,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if we could compensate or outright avoid these \neffects, there are many other potential sources  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 563920,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of noise. natural vibrations in the experimental \nsetup, seismic noise, and even gravitational noise  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 569920,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from nearby massive objects. A recent example of \na successful teensy tiny Cavendish experiment came  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 576320,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from a group of physicists in Vienna. They built a \nCavendish setup with tiny gold spheres a thousand  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 583920,
    "dDurationMs": 9040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "times lighter than the original lead source mass \nat under 100 mg. These 2 mm gold beads kept a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 592960,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pendulum rod suspended on a wispy silica thread. \nThis experiment was all about minimizing external  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 599760,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "noise and interference. As with typical modern \nCavendish experiments, this one was conducted in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 606160,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a high vacuum and the masses were carefully \ndischarged here using ionized nitrogen. A  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 612080,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "conductive Faraday shield between test and source \nensured no electromagnetic interactions between  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 618640,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the balls. The tiny gravitational field they were \ntrying to measure between the balls which were  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 624320,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "separated by about 2 and 12 mm was no stronger \nthan that of a grad student standing 2 and 1/2  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 630960,
    "dDurationMs": 8400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "m away or a vianese tram rumbling by on the street \noutside. To help quieten the gravitational noise,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 639360,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the experiments were done mostly between midnight \nand 5:00 a.m. during the quiet Christmas season.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 646160,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And I guess grad students were positioned \nstrategically and asked to remain motionless  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 651440,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on top of asking them to stay up all night and \nto miss the holiday. Even with these precautions,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 655920,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more cleverness was needed to make the \nmeasurement. Now Henry Cavendish directly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 661280,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "measured his displacement of the torsion pendulum \narms by seeing how far they moved. But in this and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 668960,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "other modern Cavendish experiments, researchers \nhave a way to massively amplify that signal.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 674160,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They oscillate the position of the source mass. So \nthe test mass experiences a varying gravitational  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 680720,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "field. The pendulum also oscillates and the \nvarying gravitational field will very slightly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 687040,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "perturb that oscillation. This allows a direct \nmeasurement of the gravitational acceleration  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 692320,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "caused by the source mass. The benefit of this \nmethod is that the researchers can watch for long  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 697840,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "periods of time as the tiny pertabbations build \nup. By integrating the signal for say half a day,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 704720,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they can detect a gravitational acceleration of 10 \nthe power of minus 10 meters per second squared.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 711200,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That's a 100 billion times smaller than what \nwe feel at Earth's surface. The gravitational  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 716720,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "constant measured in this experiment turned out \nto be consistent with the known gravitational  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 721680,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "constant as measured by Cavendish and later \nexperiments. There was a difference of around  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 726000,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "9% but that's within the experimental uncertainty \nof this setup. So, the laws of gravity may not be  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 732320,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "too different for these sub hundred milligram \nmasses, but since the gold balls used here are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 739040,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "more comparable to a baseball in size than \nto an atom, that may not be too surprising.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 744960,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It's impossible to know exactly how small we need \nto go to see if the strength of gravity changes,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 751120,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if it even does. But this team feels that it \nshould at least be possible to get down to the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 756960,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "plank mass nearly 10,000 times smaller than the \ncurrent experiment with several significant but  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 763520,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "plausible refinements of the methods. If we wanted \nto get these experiments down to truly quantum  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 769280,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "scales, then we need to go down another 9 to 12 \norders of magnitude. That may not be possible  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 774160,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with the Casemir setup, but other approaches \nlike levitating nano particles or cryogenic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 779360,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "suspension may make it possible. So, we've been \ntalking about measuring gravity down to the near  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 784240,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quantum scale. The other way we can go is try \nto observe quantum effects on as large a scale  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 789360,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as possible. One of the most defining of quantum \nphenomena is quantum entanglement in which a pair  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 795440,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of quantum objects can become correlated with each \nother in a way that defies classical explanation.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 800800,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We typically think of entanglement as existing \nbetween truly quantum scale entities. For example,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 806480,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a pair of electrons may have entangled spins. Each \nspin direction undefined when taken separately,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 812240,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but those entangled spins are defined relative \nto each other. For example, as having opposite  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 818320,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "directions, entanglement should exist between \nlarge systems. But the larger the system,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 824160,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the harder it is to observe. The correlations \nbetween individual particles can be smeared  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 829280,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out and lost to the surrounding environment in \na process called decoherence. Decoherence plays  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 835600,
    "dDurationMs": 7360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a huge role, perhaps the entire role in making \nthe quantum classical. And we could understand  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 842960,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that process a lot better if we could somehow \nobserve entanglement in a macroscopic property  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 848080,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of a macroscopic system. One of the most promising \napproaches is in the field of optochanics. Imagine  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 855120,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a laser light bouncing between two mirrors. We \ncall this an optochanical cavity. The mirrors  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 861120,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in this case are suspended so they can oscillate \nforward and back. A photon from the laser hitting  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 866320,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the mirrors will transfer momentum that depends on \nthe photon frequency. That sets up an oscillation  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 872080,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the mirrors which in turn changes the size of \nthe cavity which in turn changes the frequency  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 876720,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the modes of light in the cavity. This leads \nto a feedback cycle in which the oscillation of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 882480,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the mirrors is correlated with the frequency \nof the photons. And done carefully enough,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 887120,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "this correlation can be a true quantum \nentanglement. This has been achieved with  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 893120,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "very very tiny mirrors. The first was in 2010 \nwith an entanglement demonstrated between a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 899440,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "light field and just a single membrane of silica \nnitride as the mirror. But in 2011, a pair of  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 906400,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mirrors was put into entanglement, not just with \nthe bouncing photons, but also with each other.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 911600,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That means two macroscopic systems in a state \nof entanglement with each other. So here we are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 918080,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "really blurring the line between the quantum and \nthe classical. But these mirrors were still tiny,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 923520,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "really messcopic. It would be nice if we could \nachieve the same result for something truly  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 929200,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "macroscopic. Of course, it would be incredibly \nexpensive to build a much larger optochanical  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 936080,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "cavity with all the precision engineering, the \nnoise mitigation tech, etc. to even attempt a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 941680,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "macroscopic optochanical entanglement measurement. \nGood thing we already built one. The laser  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 946960,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "interroter gravitational wave observatory, LIGO, \nwas designed to detect space-time ripples, but in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 953360,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "principle could be used to detect correlations \nin the oscillations of the mirrors that point  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 958480,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to true entanglement between these genuinely \nmacroscopic objects. Many of the challenges for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 964880,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "doing this with LIGO have already been solved. \nIn order to detect actual gravitational waves,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 970640,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "incredible work was done to minimize random noise, \nto flag and remove seismic vibrations, and even  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 976400,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "remove spirious gravitational signals. The major \noutstanding challenge is dealing with a special  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 982720,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "type of noise that can confuse the entanglement \nsignal. This is non-marovian noise which unlike  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 989200,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "regular noise develops correlations over time \nsort of like a memory that can be confused for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 995600,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the entanglement source correlations. Motivated by \nthe goal of teasing out any signs of entanglement  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1002160,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in LIGO, in 2024, a group of researchers went back \nthrough the LIGO's data, now equipped with new and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1008640,
    "dDurationMs": 8080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "improved models to account for the non-makovia \nnoise, and they haven't found anything yet.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1016720,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But with better noise modeling and more \nintegration time, we may spot the quantum whisper  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1023040,
    "dDurationMs": 7680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of entanglement between LIGO's 40 kg mirrors which \nspan its 4 km arms. Detecting entanglement between  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1030720,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "classical or semic-class objects can help us \nunderstand the boundary between the quantum  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1035440,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the classical. The Cavendish experiment we \nstarted with was about exploring the quantum  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1040560,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nature of gravity. Now the holy grail would be to \nbring these goals together to detect entanglement  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1046640,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "between systems in which the entanglement is \nactually mediated by gravity. If gravity can  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1052960,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "do this, it means that gravity itself must \nhave quantum properties. Now we did look at  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1058480,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one approach a while back with a stern-gerlach \ntype experiment in which nano diamonds in a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1064720,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "super position of positions nudge at each other \ngravitationally and so become quantum entangled.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1071600,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there are other proposals that don't require \nthe super position of positions. For example,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1077920,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "putting test masses in a superp position of spin \nstates whose energy difference then leads to a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1083920,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "difference in the gravitational fields that they \ngenerate. or even a real Cavendish-like experiment  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1090560,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in which a pair of oscillating pendula develop \nnon-class correlations over long periods of time  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1096560,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that can only have arisen from a gravitationally \nmediated quantum connection. All of these ideas  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1102640,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are still in early phases from thought experiment \nto active planning. The technological hurdles are  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1109200,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "major but the crazy thing is that the hurdles are \njust technological and there are clear paths to  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1115120,
    "dDurationMs": 8160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "solving them. This is what's so cool. We will soon \nhave a lab bench experiment that can start to map  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1123280,
    "dDurationMs": 7600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the quantum classical divide and even observe the \nquantum behavior of gravity. Physics is cool. Just  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1130880,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a couple hundred years from lead balls suspended \nfrom wires to the quantum nature of spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1137440,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to Anydesk for supporting \nPBS. Anydesk was created because,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1141280,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "whether you’re in a remote location or just \nworking from home, the need for reliable,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1146400,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "secure communication and data transfer remains \nimportant. With Anydesk, secure, direct file  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1153280,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "transfer bypasses the cloud entirely, allowing \nyou to move large media files, benchmarks, logs,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1159760,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or firmware updates between systems. And if \nyou’re working on servers or older systems,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1165440,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with remote server monitoring and CMS access you \ncan control dashboards and monitor web servers  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1171120,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "directly from your phone or tablet. Plus it’s \navailable across all major platforms and has  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1176000,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "backwards compatibility with older systems in \ncase your data is running from legacy systems.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1181520,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Anydesk is available for personal use, but if your \nresearch requires a lot of global coordination,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1187680,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Anydesk offers custom-tailored business \nplans. To learn more about Anydesk,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1192480,
    "dDurationMs": 23280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "go to anydesk.com/spacetime or check \nout the link in the description."
    } ]
  } ]
}
